# Netlify redirect rules, copied verbatim into _site/ at build time.
#
# Every page moved from a flat URL to a locale-prefixed one in the multilingual
# migration. These 301s preserve the existing search rankings and any inbound
# links; without them the old URLs 404.
#
# Order matters: Netlify applies the FIRST matching rule, so the specific
# /blog/:slug/ rule has to sit above anything broader.
#
# Deliberately NOT doing Accept-Language negotiation on "/". Google discourages
# automatic language redirects, and they trap a reader who wants the other
# language. "/" goes to the default locale and the language switcher does the
# rest.

# --- Blog posts (most specific first) ---
/blog/:slug/         /en-ca/blog/:slug/    301
/blog/:slug          /en-ca/blog/:slug/    301

# --- Content sections that moved under /about/ ---
#
# The Foundations and Legal sections were published at the top of the locale
# before they became subsections of About. Splat rules cover every page in each
# section — /en-ca/legal/privacy/ -> /en-ca/about/legal/privacy/ — and the two
# bare-section rules above them catch the index, which :splat matches as empty.
#
# English only: the French tree keeps mentions-legales/ at the top of the
# locale, so its URLs did not move.
/en-ca/foundations/          /en-ca/about/foundations/          301
/en-ca/legal/                /en-ca/about/legal/                301
/en-ca/foundations/*         /en-ca/about/foundations/:splat    301
/en-ca/legal/*               /en-ca/about/legal/:splat          301

# --- Top-level pages ---
/index.html          /en-ca/               301
/about.html          /en-ca/about/         301
/services.html       /en-ca/services/      301
/contact.html        /en-ca/contact/       301
/free-audit.html     /en-ca/free-audit/    301
/blog.html           /en-ca/blog/          301
/privacy.html        /en-ca/privacy/       301

# --- Root ---
/                    /en-ca/               301
